{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773184403,"nonce":"NfDat5AjdI64Efql+PpzCPl9+08=","title":"Issue scanner: persistent SQLite cache with poll + write-through invalidation","message":"Currently the issue scanner keeps an in-memory cache (60s TTL) populated by git-bug CLI invocations. This cache dies on daemon restart, cannot be queried via SQL, and does not store descriptions (those are fetched on-demand per issue, N+1).\n\n## Target architecture\n\nSQLite as persistent read cache; git-bug as authoritative write-path.\n\n### Storage\n\nNew `issues` table (migration) with columns mirroring GitBugIssue fields plus:\n- `description TEXT` — cached from getIssueDetail, avoids N+1 on-demand fetches\n- `parent_id TEXT` — derived from `parent:\u003chuman_id\u003e` label at sync time (see related issue for label convention)\n- `synced_at INTEGER` — timestamp of last git-bug sync for this issue\n\n### Sync strategy: option 3 (poll + write-through)\n\nTwo-layer approach:\n\n**Layer 1 — background poll (option 1):**\nA periodic background job (interval TBD, suggested 5 min) runs `git-bug bug --format=json` per repo and upserts results into the `issues` table. This is the universal correctness layer — it catches all changes regardless of source: CLI-direct writes, another user's git-bug changes arriving via git pull, ref repairs, etc.\n\n**Layer 2 — write-through invalidation (option 3 fast path):**\nWhenever Mandible itself calls git-bug (label new, status, comment, etc.), it also immediately updates the corresponding SQLite row. This gives the local user 0-latency reads for their own actions without waiting for the next poll cycle.\n\nMulti-user correctness: both layers work correctly. Remote writes from other users (synced via git) are caught by layer 1. Layer 2 is a UX optimization, not a correctness requirement.\n\n### Benefits over current model\n\n- Warm cache on daemon restart (no cold-start latency)\n- SQL queries across entities: `SELECT * FROM issues WHERE parent_id = ? AND repo_id = ?` instead of client-side array filtering\n- Descriptions cached: getIssueDetail becomes a DB read for warm cache hits\n- Enables richer cross-entity queries (issues × sessions × relations)\n- parent_id as a proper column derived from labels at sync time\n\n### Notes\n\n- File watchers (FSEvents/inotify on .git/refs/bugs/) are explicitly NOT the right approach: unreliable for packed git objects, no per-issue granularity, extra dependency, same eventual outcome as polling\n- The git-bug label approach for parent-child relationships (parent:\u003chuman_id\u003e labels) is the authoritative storage layer; SQLite is derived/cached from it","files":null}]}